}

data gunbc_srv1_converged_slot_throttle_observation_note: String = "THE OBSERVATION THAT SUPERSEDED THE 14GiB ROW, CARRYING THE LINE IT WAS TAKEN UNDER (gunbc.ci_floor_measurement gunbc_ci_floor_run_peak_receipt_carries_its_own_line_note: a peak is evidence only together with the armed line, or it silently re-interprets itself when the declaration moves). Read on srv1 2026-08-17 by direct systemctl show plus memory.events and memory.peak on each slot's cgroup.\n\nALL FIVE srv1 SLOTS CARRIED THE DECLARED 14GiB/13GiB, which is the fact that refuted the premise this change opened on — srv1 was believed to be sitting at 16GiB/15GiB like srv2-03, and it was not; it was converged. srv1-01/02/03 peaked at 2.08/10.06/8.17GiB with zero events of any kind. srv1-04 and srv1-05 both peaked at EXACTLY 13.00GiB — the armed MemoryHigh to the byte — against 829 and 44803 memory.high events, zero memory.max events and zero oom_kills.\n\nA PEAK EQUAL TO THE ARMED THROTTLE LINE IS A CENSORED LOWER BOUND, not demand: the same reading gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_ruling_note applied to the four 15.0GiB pins in its 186-sample calibration. Two of five slots pinned is a different regime from four of 186, and the difference is that the 2026-08-05 convergence moved the line down onto the workload. Zero oom_kills does not mean the envelope fit — reclaim and swap absorbed the overage, and the host carried 10GiB of swap in use at the same reading.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT EVIDENCE FOR: it does not measure the floor's demand, because a censored bound cannot. The demand figure is gunbc_floor_measured_peak_observation, measured off-host by the claim-scope instrument. This observation establishes only that the declared line was being hit and held."

data gunbc_srv1_04_converged_slot_throttle_observation: ConvergedSlotThrottleObservation = ConvergedSlotThrottleObservation {
  runner_unit: "actions-runner@srv1-04.service",
  armed_memory_max: byte_size(15032385536),
  armed_memory_high: byte_size(13958643712),
  memory_peak: byte_size(13958643712),
  memory_high_events: 829,
  memory_max_events: 0,
  oom_kill_events: 0,
  probed_at: "2026-08-17",
}

data gunbc_srv1_05_converged_slot_throttle_observation: ConvergedSlotThrottleObservation = ConvergedSlotThrottleObservation {
  runner_unit: "actions-runner@srv1-05.service",
  armed_memory_max: byte_size(15032385536),
  armed_memory_high: byte_size(13958643712),
  memory_peak: byte_size(13958643712),
  memory_high_events: 44803,
  memory_max_events: 0,
  oom_kill_events: 0,
  probed_at: "2026-08-17",
}

fn converged_slot_is_pinned_at_armed_throttle_line(obs: ConvergedSlotThrottleObservation) -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: obs.memory_peak) == byte_size_count(b: obs.armed_memory_high)
    && obs.memory_high_events > 0
}

data gunbc_floor_measured_peak_observation_note: String = "THE FLOOR'S PEAK, MEASURED IN BYTES FROM cgroup memory.peak WITH ITS ARMED LINE BESIDE IT — no longer an interpretation of a rendered string. Read on srv2 2026-08-17 across all five slots, each carrying memory.high at 16106127360 and memory.high events at ZERO, so every figure below is an UNCENSORED peak rather than a throttle pin (the censored-bound distinction gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_ruling_note turns on).\n\nTHE THREE HIGHEST CLEAN PEAKS: srv2-02 at 15509315584 bytes, srv2-05 at 15501008896, srv2-04 at 15474360320. The carrier takes the maximum, 15509315584, because a per-slot ceiling must hold the heaviest observed slot and not a central tendency.\n\nTHIS RETIRES THE UNIT AMBIGUITY BY OBSERVATION RATHER THAN BY PREFERENCE, which is the point. The prior revision of this row carried a hand-typed 15600000000 — the DECIMAL reading of a claim-scope receipt rendered as '15.6 GB' — and openly conceded that the BINARY reading (16750372454) would make the wall conjunct fail. A load-bearing gate hinging on an author's choice between two readings is the 2026-08-01 test-oracle ruling's concern, correctly raised in review on this PR.\n\nThe readings are now DISCRIMINATED, not chosen. Under the binary reading demand would be 16750372454 bytes, which EXCEEDS the 16106127360 line these five slots were armed at — so they would be pinned with nonzero memory.high events. All five report zero. The binary reading is therefore refuted by measurement, and the decimal reading is independently corroborated: the measured maximum sits within 0.6 percent of it. The rendered string is retained as corroboration only; nothing gates on it.\n\nWHAT THIS IS STILL NOT: a bound on the floor's DEMAND. A clean peak is a lower bound on what the workload wanted on the runs observed, and srv4-05 was separately pinned at exactly 15.00GiB with 1789 events on the same day, so at least one slot on this fleet wants MORE than the 15GiB line. The 16GiB/15GiB row is therefore a strict improvement over 14GiB/13GiB and NOT a claim that throttling is eliminated. Dissolve-on: a floor-run receipt series emitting peak and armed line per run, which would replace this single-day cross-sectional read with a distribution."

data gunbc_floor_measured_peak_observation: ByteSize = byte_size(15509315584)

data gunbc_floor_measured_peak_armed_high: ByteSize = byte_size(16106127360)

data gunbc_floor_measured_peak_reported_decimal_corroboration: ByteSize = byte_size(15600000000)

fn gunbc_floor_measured_peak_is_uncensored() -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_measured_peak_observation)
    < byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_measured_peak_armed_high)
}

data gunbc_srv2_03_converge_floor_fit_note: String = "SUPERSEDED BY THE 2026-08-17 RULING, RETAINED BECAUSE ITS QUESTION WAS ANSWERED RATHER THAN ABANDONED. This note asked whether converging srv2-03 DOWN to a 14GiB/13GiB declaration would be the first enforcement of the modeled envelope against the CI floor, and recorded that the floor's compliance with that envelope was UNMEASURED — neither known-good nor known-bad.\n\nIT IS NOW MEASURED AND THE FLOOR DID NOT FIT. gunbc_floor_measured_peak_observation is an uncensored 15509315584-byte peak, above the 14GiB MemoryMax the old row declared. And the convergence this note warned about was actually performed — on srv1, not srv2-03 — where two slots then sat pinned at exactly 13.00GiB with 829 and 44803 memory.high events (gunbc_srv1_converged_slot_throttle_observation). So the answer is not hypothetical: enforcing that envelope degraded the consumer, and the declaration moved to 16GiB/15GiB rather than the fleet moving down to 14/13.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR srv2-03 SPECIFICALLY: it is no longer drifted on this axis at all — its 16GiB/15GiB readback now equals the declaration, so there is no convergence to perform and no first-enforcement event pending (gunbc_srv2_03_drift_dissolved_by_ruling_note). The FloorCgroupLeafEventAtEntry snapshot below stays as the zero-event baseline it always was.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: this note is not evidence for reconciling a declaration toward a readback, and never was. The row moved on a floor-peak measurement; srv2-03 agreeing with the result is a coincidence, recorded as one in gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_ruling_note."


data gunbc_srv2_03_drift_dissolved_by_ruling_note: String = "srv2-03'S DRIFT IS DISSOLVED BY THE 2026-08-17 RULING, AND THAT IS A CONSEQUENCE TO RECORD RATHER THAN A RESULT TO CLAIM. The host was drifted 2GiB above a 14GiB/13GiB declaration; the declaration moved to 16GiB/15GiB for reasons having nothing to do with srv2-03 (a floor-peak measurement plus an observed throttle pin on srv1, gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_ruling_note), and the observation now equals the target. NOTHING WAS APPLIED TO srv2-03 and no convergence work was performed on it — the gap closed from the declaration's side.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, AND THIS NOTE IS ITS RECEIPT: srv2_03_effective_envelope_drifts_two_gib_above_declaration and srv2_03_converge_would_first_enforce_modeled_envelope became FALSE and are DELETED, together with the two witnesses that asserted them. They were retired rather than negated. Inverting them would assert 'srv2-03 agrees with the declaration', a claim about one host's convergence state on one day that decays the instant anything is applied.\n\nTHE GENERAL RELATIONS ARE UNCHANGED and remain this module's authority on readback refusal: runner_slot_effective_cap_drifts_from_declaration and gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_refuses_effective_readback_reconciliation still refuse exactly as before. But srv2-03 was their only discriminating input and it has stopped discriminating, so this change would have left them UNWITNESSED — the silent half of the failure. gunbc_synthetic_drifted_cap_observation replaces it deliberately as a SYNTHETIC fixture rather than another host reading, so no future convergence can quietly green it the way convergence just greened srv2-03."

data gunbc_synthetic_drifted_cap_observation: RunnerSlotEffectiveCapObservation = RunnerSlotEffectiveCapMeasured {
  runner_unit: "synthetic-drifted-slot.service",
  memory_max: byte_size(21474836480),
  memory_high: byte_size(20401094656),
  probed_at: "synthetic",
}

fn gunbc_readback_refusal_stays_witnessed_by_synthetic_drift() -> Bool {
  runner_slot_effective_cap_drifts_from_declaration(obs: gunbc_synthetic_drifted_cap_observation)
    && gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_refuses_effective_readback_reconciliation(
         obs: gunbc_synthetic_drifted_cap_observation
       )
}

fn srv2_03_floor_leaf_cgroup_events_at_entry_are_zero() -> Bool {
  gunbc_srv2_03_floor_t5_leaf_cgroup_events_at_entry.memory_high_events == 0
    && gunbc_srv2_03_floor_t5_leaf_cgroup_events_at_entry.memory_max_events == 0
    && gunbc_srv2_03_floor_t5_leaf_cgroup_events_at_entry.oom_kill_events == 0
}


fn runner_slot_effective_memory_max_drifts_from_declaration(effective: ByteSize) -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: effective) != byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max)
}

fn runner_slot_effective_memory_high_drifts_from_declaration(effective: ByteSize) -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: effective) != byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_high)
}

fn runner_slot_effective_cap_drifts_from_declaration(obs: RunnerSlotEffectiveCapObservation) -> Bool {
  match obs {
    RunnerSlotEffectiveCapMeasured { runner_unit: _, memory_max: max, memory_high: high, probed_at: _ } =>
      runner_slot_effective_memory_max_drifts_from_declaration(effective: max)
        || runner_slot_effective_memory_high_drifts_from_declaration(effective: high)
    RunnerSlotEffectiveCapUnmeasured => false
  }
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_refuses_effective_readback_reconciliation(obs: RunnerSlotEffectiveCapObservation) -> Bool {
  match obs {
    RunnerSlotEffectiveCapMeasured { runner_unit: _, memory_max: _, memory_high: _, probed_at: _ } =>
      runner_slot_effective_cap_drifts_from_declaration(obs: obs)
    RunnerSlotEffectiveCapUnmeasured => false
  }
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_wall_oracle_note: String = "WHAT THIS WALL USED TO ASSERT WAS ITS OWN INPUT. Three of its five conjuncts compared gunbc_runner_slot_desired's fields to byte literals retyped from that same declaration twenty lines above, in the same file — measure() == measure() with the equality maintained by hand, which the 2026-08-01 test-oracle ruling names exactly: automate the literal's update and the assertion collapses to nothing, so the manual update WAS the assertion's entire content. It could only ever fail when someone edited one copy and forgot the other, and it would have gone red on this change for that reason and no other.\n\nThe conjuncts that replace them are relations between INDEPENDENTLY derived quantities, so each can fail for a reason worth knowing. The max−1GiB relation is the authored MemoryHigh law from the 2026-07-11 ruling and it is checkable without naming either number. The MemoryHigh ≥ floor conjunct is the load-bearing one and it is NEW: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget is a MEASURED refusal threshold (12GiB, from the doomed-witness receipts below) that claim_executor enforces at arm time, and MemoryHigh is precisely the budget the run-time governor arms against — so shrinking the slot cap below it would ship a fleet on which every floor run refuses itself. Nothing connected those two rows before; the 14GiB ruling is what made the distance between them small enough to matter.\n\nTHE FLOOR-PEAK CONJUNCT ADDED 2026-08-17 is the second load-bearing relation and the one that would have prevented this change's own hazard: gunbc_floor_measured_peak_observation is an independently measured demand figure, and requiring MemoryHigh >= it makes a per-slot row the floor cannot fit in UNWRITABLE rather than discoverable at 3am on a converged host. It is a relation between two independently derived quantities in exactly the sense this note demands — neither number is retyped from the other — and it fails for a reason worth knowing: either the declaration shrank below measured demand, or demand grew past the declaration.\n\nITS INPUT IS GUARDED BY gunbc_floor_measured_peak_is_uncensored, which is not decoration: a peak equal to its own armed line is a THROTTLE PIN, a censored lower bound, and gating a ceiling on one would silently size the fleet to whatever line happened to be in force rather than to demand — the precise confusion this module spent two rulings distinguishing. Requiring the observation to sit strictly below the line it was taken under makes a censored figure unusable as the gate's input rather than merely discouraged.\n\nGuaranteed mode is deliberately NOT asserted here any more. runner_slice_cap is derived as slots × MemoryMax, so Σ ≤ cap is true by construction and a check of it would be the vacuous restatement DESIGN 5 says to prefer construction over. gunbc_runner_slot_oversubscription_within_slice survives as the general relation for callers holding two independent numbers."

fn gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_wall_holds() -> Bool {
  let per_slot = gunbc_runner_slot_desired()
  let gibibyte_bytes = 1073741824
  (hardware_thread_count_value(t: gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot()) * byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_memory_per_core) >= byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_max))
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_high) + gibibyte_bytes == byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_max))
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_high) >= byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget))
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_high) >= byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_measured_peak_observation))
    && gunbc_floor_measured_peak_is_uncensored()
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_swap_max) > byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_max))
    && all(
      gunbc_committed_runner_slot_allocations(),
      a =>
        a.slots_per_host > 0
          && gunbc_runner_slot_oversubscription_within_slice(
               slots: a.slots_per_host,
               per_slot: a.per_slot.memory_max,
               slice_cap: a.runner_slice_cap
             )
    )
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_live_cap_dissolve_on: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.runner_slot_allocation",
    decl_name: "gunbc_runner_slot_desired",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data gunbc_floor_armed_budget_doomed_exit_137_outcome: FloorOutcome = FloorFailed {
  class: Structural { reason: "Process completed with exit code 137." }
}

type FloorArmedBudgetDoomedWitness
  = FloorArmedBudgetDoomedMeasured {
      run_id: String,
      runner_unit: String,
      armed_budget: ByteSize,
      budget_source: String,
      floor_outcome: FloorOutcome,
    }
  | FloorArmedBudgetDoomedUnmeasured

type FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassWitness
  = FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassMeasured {
      run_id: String,
      runner_unit: String,
      armed_budget: ByteSize,
      floor_peak_post: ByteSize,
      floor_outcome: FloorOutcome,
    }
  | FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassUnmeasured

data gunbc_floor_armed_budget_doomed_witness_receipts: List<FloorArmedBudgetDoomedWitness> = [
  FloorArmedBudgetDoomedMeasured {
    run_id: "29834380839",
    runner_unit: "srv3-05",
    armed_budget: byte_size(6987137024),
    budget_source: "/proc/meminfo MemAvailable",
    floor_outcome: gunbc_floor_armed_budget_doomed_exit_137_outcome,
  },
  FloorArmedBudgetDoomedMeasured {
    run_id: "29845210061",
    runner_unit: "srv3-02",
    armed_budget: byte_size(5971566592),
    budget_source: "/proc/meminfo MemAvailable",
    floor_outcome: gunbc_floor_armed_budget_doomed_exit_137_outcome,
  },
]

data gunbc_floor_armed_budget_success_class_receipt: FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassWitness = FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassMeasured {
  run_id: "29835983525",
  runner_unit: "srv1-05",
  armed_budget: byte_size(16106127360),
  floor_peak_post: byte_size(16108544000),
  floor_outcome: NoFailure,
}

data gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget: ByteSize = byte_size(12884901888)

data gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget_disposition: Disposition = Terminal {
  reason: "MEASURED minimum viable armed budget for the CI floor (operator ruling 2026-07-21, post-#6995): 12GiB (12884901888 bytes) is the conservative lower bound of the healthy capped-box success class (~12.5-16GiB floor_peak_post), NOT the typical peak. Derivation: doomed witnesses gunbc_floor_armed_budget_doomed_witness_receipts prove armed budgets 5.97-6.99GiB at arm time (MemAvailable on crowded uncapped srv3) cannot complete the floor walk - runs 29834380839 and 29845210061 died in batch witness phase after ~30min; success-class receipt gunbc_floor_armed_budget_success_class_receipt (run 29835983525 post-#6995, srv1-05, armed_budget=15GiB memory.high, floor_peak_post=16108544000) proves the healthy class sits above this bound. bright-seal #6999 fill-deferral is perf-only (entry-closure memo); coordinate via gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget_dissolve_on if a future change cuts mature index residency. Consumed by claim_executor arm-time FloorBudgetBelowMinimumFootprint refusal (fail-fast, requeue-able)."
}

data gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget_dissolve_on: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.runner_slot_allocation",
    decl_name: "gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

fn gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget_bytes() -> ByteSize {
  gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget
}

fn witness_floor_minimum_viable_above_doomed_receipts() -> Bool {
  all(
    gunbc_floor_armed_budget_doomed_witness_receipts,
    r =>
      match r {
        FloorArmedBudgetDoomedMeasured { run_id: _, runner_unit: _, armed_budget: budget, budget_source: _, floor_outcome: _ } =>
          byte_size_count(b: budget) < byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget)
        FloorArmedBudgetDoomedUnmeasured => false
      }
  )
}

fn witness_floor_minimum_viable_below_success_class() -> Bool {
  match gunbc_floor_armed_budget_success_class_receipt {
    FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassMeasured { run_id: _, runner_unit: _, armed_budget: budget, floor_peak_post: _, floor_outcome: _ } =>
      byte_size_count(b: budget) >= byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget)
    FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassUnmeasured => false
  }
}

fn witness_floor_armed_budget_doomed_outcomes_are_failures() -> Bool {
  all(
    gunbc_floor_armed_budget_doomed_witness_receipts,
    r =>
      match r {
        FloorArmedBudgetDoomedMeasured { run_id: _, runner_unit: _, armed_budget: _, budget_source: _, floor_outcome: o } =>
          floor_outcome_blocks_merge(o: o)
        FloorArmedBudgetDoomedUnmeasured => false
      }
  )
}

fn witness_floor_armed_budget_success_outcome_is_no_failure() -> Bool {
  match gunbc_floor_armed_budget_success_class_receipt {
    FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassMeasured { run_id: _, runner_unit: _, armed_budget: _, floor_peak_post: _, floor_outcome: o } =>
      match o {
        NoFailure => true
        FloorFailed { class: _ } => false
      }
    FloorArmedBudgetSuccessClassUnmeasured => false
  }
}

fn gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget_wall_holds() -> Bool {
  witness_floor_minimum_viable_above_doomed_receipts()
    && witness_floor_minimum_viable_below_success_class()
    && witness_floor_armed_budget_doomed_outcomes_are_failures()
    && witness_floor_armed_budget_success_outcome_is_no_failure()
    && byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget) == byte_size_count(b: byte_size(12884901888))
}

type RunnerSlotPurpose
  = SlotServesGitHubActions
  | SlotServesFabricExecution

// THE VARIANT IS NOT NAMED RunnerSlotMember BECAUSE THAT NAME IS TAKEN, AND THE COLLISION IS
// SILENT AT THE SITE. gunbc.runner_slot_provision already declares a plain record type
// RunnerSlotMember { instance_name, slot_dir } -- a different concept entirely, a materialized
// install tree rather than a committed allocation. Names resolve against one flat whole-tree
// namespace, so a coproduct variant spelled the same way resolved to THAT product, and the floor
// refused: "if branches resolve to incompatible types: Coproduct(RunnerSlotMembership) vs
// Product(RunnerSlotMember)" -- one arm the coproduct, the other someone else's record, in a
// function whose arms are all variants of one type.
//
// RECORDED BECAUSE NOTHING LOCAL SHOWS IT. The source reads as an ordinary coproduct, every witness
// ran green under the interpreter, and `gunbc compile` typechecks clean; only the floor sees it. A
// future rename back to RunnerSlotMember would silently rebind to the provision record again.
//
// An earlier revision of this comment blamed a longest-common-prefix effect between the variant and
// RunnerSlotMembership. That was a guess that fit the symptom, and it was wrong -- the cause is an
// ordinary duplicate declaration, found by grepping the tree rather than by reasoning about the
// resolver. The wrong explanation is kept here because it is the plausible one and the next reader
// will reach for it too.
type RunnerSlotMembership
  = CommittedSlotMember { identity: RunnerSlotIdentity, purpose: RunnerSlotPurpose }
  | SlotOutsideCommittedPopulation { identity: RunnerSlotIdentity, committed_width: Int }

// THE CARVE IS AN EXACT IDENTITY, NOT AN INDEX ARITHMETIC RULE, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS
// MODULE GOT THAT WRONG IN THE EXACT WAY IT WAS WRITTEN TO PREVENT. It derived purpose by comparing
// the index against a count -- fabric if slot_index > committed - fabric_count -- which reproduces
// count-as-authority one level below the enumeration where it was just removed. Two measured
// consequences: the fabric slot MOVED with the width (srv3 at width 7 would have made srv3-07 the
// fabric slot and handed srv3-06 back to Actions, silently relabelling a slot that may be running),
// and it answered SlotServesComputeFabric for identities that are not slots at all -- executed:
// runner_slot_purpose(srv1, 6) and (srv3, 99) both returned fabric, though srv1 is five wide and
// neither index is a committed member.
//
// So the fabric member is AUTHORED as an identity and the counts are PROJECTIONS over it. srv3-06
// stays srv3-06 when the width moves, and if the width ever falls below 6 the slot leaves the
// committed population and says so, rather than the fabric role sliding onto srv3-05.
data srv3_fabric_canary_slot_index: Nat = 6

fn srv3_fabric_canary_slot() -> RunnerSlotIdentity {
  RunnerSlotIdentity { host: operator_host_srv3, slot_index: srv3_fabric_canary_slot_index }
}

// srv3 CARRIES THE ONE FABRIC EXECUTION SLOT AND srv1 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT (operator, 2026-08-21).
// The control plane is taking a temporary srv1 slice, so the executor is placed on a DIFFERENT host:
// a control plane and the capacity it schedules failing together is the common-mode failure the
// placement exists to avoid. srv3 over srv4 because both admit 6 at memory and srv4 additionally
// carries the apply-time disk preflight, so srv3 has fewer axes able to refuse it.
//
// THIS IS THE EXECUTION MEMBER ONLY. The srv1 control-plane slice is NOT a member of this list and
// must not become one: an allocatable executor and a statically pinned bootstrap have opposite
// liveness properties -- the executor is selected by Demand and appears in a SupplierOffer, the control
// plane is placed by host convergence, never appears in a SupplierOffer, and must not need a Grant to
// start. Overloading one variant across both would erase exactly that distinction, which is why
// the variant is named SlotServesFabricExecution rather than SlotServesComputeFabric.
fn fabric_execution_slot_identities() -> List<RunnerSlotIdentity> {
  [srv3_fabric_canary_slot()]
}

fn identity_is_fabric_execution_member(identity: RunnerSlotIdentity) -> Bool {
  identity_in_list(identity: identity, members: fabric_execution_slot_identities())
}

fn identity_in_list(identity: RunnerSlotIdentity, members: List<RunnerSlotIdentity>) -> Bool {
  fold(
    members,
    init: false,
    f: (found, m) => found || runner_slot_identity_equal(a: m, b: identity)
  )
}

// MEMBERSHIP PRECEDES PURPOSE. Asking what a slot is FOR only means something once it is a slot,
// and the committed width is what decides that. An identity outside the range is refused with the
// width it was measured against rather than being classified, so "is this a committed member" and
// "what is its purpose" stay two questions with two answers.
fn committed_slot_identities(host: HostIdentity) -> List<RunnerSlotIdentity> {
  map(
    nat_range_inclusive(lo: 1, hi: gunbc_runner_slots_per_host(host: host)),
    i => RunnerSlotIdentity { host: host, slot_index: i }
  )
}

fn runner_slot_membership(host: HostIdentity, slot_index: Nat) -> RunnerSlotMembership {
  let identity = RunnerSlotIdentity { host: host, slot_index: slot_index }
  let committed = gunbc_runner_slots_per_host(host: host)
  if slot_index > committed || slot_index < 1 {
    SlotOutsideCommittedPopulation { identity: identity, committed_width: committed }
  } else if identity_is_fabric_execution_member(identity: identity) {
    CommittedSlotMember { identity: identity, purpose: SlotServesFabricExecution }
  } else {
    CommittedSlotMember { identity: identity, purpose: SlotServesGitHubActions }
  }
}

fn count_fabric_members(identities: List<RunnerSlotIdentity>) -> Int {
  fold(
    identities,
    init: 0,
    f: (n, id) => n + (if identity_is_fabric_execution_member(identity: id) { 1 } else { 0 })
  )
}

// THE COUNTS ARE PROJECTIONS OF THE POPULATION, NEVER THE AUTHORITY FOR IT. That direction is the
// whole point: a width can say six slots exist, but it cannot say the sixth belongs to the fabric
// while the first five are GitHub runners. Read the members, then count them.
fn host_fabric_slot_count(host: HostIdentity) -> Int {
  count_fabric_members(identities: committed_slot_identities(host: host))
}

fn host_github_slot_count(host: HostIdentity) -> Int {
  gunbc_runner_slots_per_host(host: host) - host_fabric_slot_count(host: host)
}
